  "query_preview": "../Monday through Thursday/...High pressure persists \nover the Gulf of Alaska, continuing to keep the region seasonably \ndry and warm. Beginning Monday, an impactful Bering Sea low \npressure system tracking east across western and central mainland \nAlaska will send several fronts across the northern half of the \nregion, bringing increasing chances of light rain through the \nweek. Expect an initial, lighter pass of showers on Tuesday, with \nprecipitation amounts increasing as a secondary front pushes in by\nWednesday morning along the NE Gulf Coast, where it is likely to \nsee the greatest amounts. While 24-hour rainfall totals are \ntrending higher with recent model runs, guidance spread remains. \nSome areas, like Yakutat, could see locally heavy rainfall \novernight Tuesday into Wednesday. \n\nThe high pressure ridge is expected to weaken as the low pressure \nsystem approaches from the west. Northwest flow along the ridge axis \nwill transport a moisture plume originating from Tropical Storm \nLowell into the northern region and could increase wind speeds along \nexposed prominent capes by Wednesday as the pressure gradient \ntightens. Also with this pattern, winds are likely to strengthen \nnear Skagway as these fronts pass. Increased cloud cover in the \nnorth will bring slightly cooler afternoon highs alongside mild \novernight lows, while the southern panhandle stays mostly sunny with \nhighs remaining in the mid to high 60s. Some guidance is starting\nto pick up on additional activity later in the week, though\noutside of the current forecast period. Stay tuned for more\ninformation as we get closer.",
